              "explanation": "The total number of observations is $N = 9$. We partition the series into $n = 3$ contiguous groups of $m = 3$ observations each:\n- **Group 1 ($t = 0, 1, 2$)**:\n  $$S_1 = \\sum_{t=0}^{2} Y_t = 14.2 + 22.8 + 29.8 = 66.8$$\n- **Group 2 ($t = 3, 4, 5$)**:\n  $$S_2 = \\sum_{t=3}^{5} Y_t = 35.5 + 40.1 + 43.8 = 119.4$$\n- **Group 3 ($t = 6, 7, 8$)**:\n  $$S_3 = \\sum_{t=6}^{8} Y_t = 46.7 + 49.1 + 51.0 = 146.8$$\n\nNow, we express each partial sum theoretically in terms of $k, a, b$:\n$$S_1 = \\sum_{t=0}^{m-1} (k + a b^t) = m k + a \\left( \frac{1 - b^m}{1 - b} \night)$$\n$$S_2 = \\sum_{t=m}^{2m-1} (k + a b^t) = m k + a b^m \\left( \frac{1 - b^m}{1 - b} \night)$$\n$$S_3 = \\sum_{t=2m}^{3m-1} (k + a b^t) = m k + a b^{2m} \\left( \frac{1 - b^m}{1 - b} \night)$$\n\nTaking successive differences to eliminate the constant term $m k$:\n$$D_1 = S_2 - S_1 = a (b^m - 1) \\left( \frac{1 - b^m}{1 - b} \night) = - a \frac{(1 - b^m)^2}{1 - b} = a \frac{(b^m - 1)^2}{1 - b}$$\n$$D_2 = S_3 - S_2 = a b^m \frac{(b^m - 1)^2}{1 - b}$$\n\nTaking the ratio of the two differences:\n$$\frac{D_2}{D_1} = \frac{S_3 - S_2}{S_2 - S_1} = b^m$$\n\nHence, the parameter $b$ is given by:\n$$b = \\left( \frac{S_3 - S_2}{S_2 - S_1} \night)^{1/m}$$\n\nOnce $b$ is determined, parameter $a$ is recovered from $D_1$:\n$$a = \frac{(S_2 - S_1)(b - 1)}{(b^m - 1)^2} = \frac{(S_2 - S_1)(b - 1)}{(b^m - 1)(b^m - 1)}$$\nMore directly:\n$$S_2 - S_1 = a b^0 (b^m - 1) \frac{b^m - 1}{b - 1} \\implies a = \frac{(S_2 - S_1)(1 - b)}{(1 - b^m)^2} \times (-1) = \frac{(S_2 - S_1)(b - 1)}{(1 - b^m)^2}$$\n\nFinally, parameter $k$ is obtained from $S_1$:\n$$k = \frac{1}{m} \\left[ S_1 - a \\left( \frac{1 - b^m}{1 - b} \night) \night]$$"
