          "content": "Modern industrial economies do not operate as isolated silos. The production of an automobile requires steel, glass, rubber, electronics, and design services; the production of steel in turn requires iron ore, coal, electrical power, and heavy transport equipment; and electricity generation consumes coal, natural gas, and turbine machinery. To analyze these dense, circular inter-industry dependencies, the Russian-American economist Wassily Leontief developed **Input-Output Analysis** in the 1930s—a breakthrough for which he was awarded the 1973 Nobel Memorial Prize in Economic Sciences.\n\nInput-Output analysis provides an empirical, matrix-algebraic formulation of general equilibrium theory. It maps the complex webs of transactions connecting all producing sectors, enabling economists to calculate how an exogenous shift in final demand for a single sector (such as an export boom, defense procurement, or green energy subsidy) ripples through the entire industrial fabric.\n\n---",
